<https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/ethnocentrism#graph> {
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/ethnocentrism/check/1> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#SelfCheckQuestion> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/ethnocentrism/check/1> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "When I called a colleague's message rude, vague or odd, was I measuring it against how people communicate where I learned to work?" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/ethnocentrism/check/1> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#questionFor> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/ethnocentrism> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/ethnocentrism/check/2> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#SelfCheckQuestion> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/ethnocentrism/check/2> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Would I rate this candidate's or manager's competence the same if their accent, style and background matched mine?" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/ethnocentrism/check/2> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#questionFor> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/ethnocentrism> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/ethnocentrism/check/3> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#SelfCheckQuestion> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/ethnocentrism/check/3> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Have I asked what this behaviour means in the other person's context before deciding what it means?" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/ethnocentrism/check/3> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#questionFor> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/ethnocentrism> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/ethnocentrism/check/4> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#SelfCheckQuestion> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/ethnocentrism/check/4> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Who on this team have I quietly been avoiding or routing around, and is the real reason a difference in style?" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/ethnocentrism/check/4> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#questionFor> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/ethnocentrism> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/ethnocentrism> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/created> "2026-11-05"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/ethnocentrism> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#Barrier> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/ethnocentrism> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Ethnocentrism" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/ethnocentrism> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "cultural ethnocentrism" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/ethnocentrism> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "ethnocentric bias" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/ethnocentrism> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "generalized ethnocentrism" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/ethnocentrism> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/intercultural-communication> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/ethnocentrism> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Treating your own culture's habits, values and ways of communicating as the normal, correct standard and judging other people's messages and behaviour against it; in workplace research it goes with lower willingness to talk across cultures and harsher judgements of out-group colleagues' competence and credibility." .
